OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Canonical-ampleness-of-compact-hyperbolic-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026", "title": "Canonical ampleness of compact hyperbolic Kähler manifolds", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Canonical-ampleness-of-compact-hyperbolic-Kahler-manifolds-September-23-2026/canonical-ampleness.pdf", "family": "051", "family_title": "Kobayashi’s canonical-ampleness conjecture", "abstract_html": "We prove that every compact connected Kähler manifold of positive complex dimension containing no nonconstant entire curve has ample canonical bundle and is projective. This resolves positively Kobayashi's canonical-ampleness conjecture in the smooth compact Kähler category.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The canonical bundle \\(K_X\\) is ample.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(X\\) is a compact connected Kähler manifold of complex dimension \\(n\\geq 1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Every holomorphic map \\(\\mathbb C\\to X\\) is constant.</p><p class=\"li\">The canonical bundle is \\(K_X=\\bigwedge^n(T^{1,0}X)^*\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A holomorphic line bundle is ample if some positive tensor power has global sections defining a holomorphic embedding into projective space.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The canonical bundle \\(K_X\\) is ample. In particular, a positive tensor power of \\(K_X\\) defines a holomorphic embedding of \\(X\\) into a complex projective space.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 9 proves that \\(K_X\\) is nef, and Proposition 11 proves\n\\[\n\\int_Xc_1(K_X)^n&gt;0.\n\\]\nApply Proposition 12 with \\(L=K_X\\). It follows that \\(K_X\\) is big and \\(X\\) is projective.</p><p>A rational curve in \\(X\\) would give a nonconstant holomorphic map\n\\(\\mathbb P^1\\to X\\) through its normalization. Its restriction to\n\\(\\mathbb C\\subset\\mathbb P^1\\) would remain nonconstant by the identity theorem, contradicting the assumption. Thus \\(X\\) contains no rational curves.</p><p>Proposition 13 now proves that \\(K_X\\) is ample. Equivalently, a sufficiently high positive tensor power is very ample, and its global sections define a holomorphic embedding into projective space.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary 10.1, Pluricanonical freeness", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The line bundle \\(K_X^{\\otimes m}\\) is generated by its global sections for every integer \\(m\\geq n+2\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(X\\) is a compact connected Brody-hyperbolic Kähler manifold of complex dimension \\(n\\geq 1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Brody hyperbolicity means that every holomorphic map \\(\\mathbb C\\to X\\) is constant.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The line bundle \\(K_X^{\\otimes m}\\) is generated by its global sections for every integer \\(m\\geq n+2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Theorem 1 makes \\(X\\) smooth projective with \\(K_X\\) ample. Apply Fujita freeness in the all-powers form cited in [23, Corollary 6.3], with \\(L=K_X\\) and exponent \\(m-1\\geq n+1\\). The resulting globally generated adjoint bundle is\n\\[\nK_X\\otimes L^{\\otimes(m-1)}=K_X^{\\otimes m}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Corollary 10.2, Finite-cover product", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a connected finite étale cover of \\(X\\) biholomorphic to \\( (D/\\Gamma_0)\\times F, \\) where:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(X\\) is a compact connected Brody-hyperbolic Kähler manifold of positive complex dimension.</p><p class=\"li\">Its ordinary universal cover is biholomorphic to a semialgebraic open subset of a complex projective variety, with openness in the ordinary complex topology.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a connected finite étale cover of \\(X\\) biholomorphic to\n\\[\n(D/\\Gamma_0)\\times F,\n\\]\nwhere:</p><p class=\"li\">\\(D\\) is a bounded symmetric domain;</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\Gamma_0\\) is a discrete group of biholomorphisms acting freely, properly discontinuously, and cocompactly on \\(D\\);</p><p class=\"li\">\\(F\\) is a simply connected compact Brody-hyperbolic projective manifold.</p><p>Either \\(D\\) or \\(F\\) may be a point.</p>", "proof": "<p>Theorem 1 makes \\(X\\) projective. The semialgebraic-cover classification cited in [24, Theorem 1.1] gives\n\\[\n\\widetilde X\\simeq D\\times\\mathbb C^m\\times F,\n\\]\nwhere \\(D\\) is a bounded symmetric domain and \\(F\\) is simply connected, normal, and projective. Smoothness of the product forces \\(F\\) to be smooth. Brody hyperbolicity passes to covers and factors, so \\(m=0\\) and \\(F\\) is Brody hyperbolic.</p><p>If \\(\\dim F&gt;0\\), Theorem 1 makes \\(K_F\\) ample. Kobayashi’s finite-automorphism theorem [18, Theorem, p. 184] gives finiteness of \\(\\operatorname{Aut}(F)\\). This also holds if \\(F\\) is a point.</p><p>For a deck transformation of \\(D\\times F\\), the first coordinate is constant on every compact connected fiber \\(F\\). Apply the same observation to its inverse: its base map is an automorphism of \\(D\\), and its fiber maps are automorphisms of \\(F\\). Since \\(D\\) is connected and \\(\\operatorname{Aut}(F)\\) finite, the fiber maps are independent of the base point. Thus the deck group \\(\\Gamma\\) acts by products.</p><p>The kernel \\(\\Gamma_0\\) of\n\\[\n\\Gamma\\to\\operatorname{Aut}(F)\n\\]\nhas finite index and acts faithfully and freely on \\(D\\). Proper discontinuity on \\(D\\) follows by applying proper discontinuity of the deck action to \\(K\\times F\\) for compact \\(K\\subset D\\); in particular, \\(\\Gamma_0\\) is discrete. Therefore\n\\[\n\\widetilde X/\\Gamma_0\\simeq(D/\\Gamma_0)\\times F\n\\]\nis a connected finite unramified holomorphic cover of \\(X\\), hence a finite étale cover of the projective manifold \\(X\\). Its compactness implies that \\(D/\\Gamma_0\\) is compact.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Proposition 3.2, Existence of a maximizing disc", "depth": 0, "snippet": "On the finite-area holomorphic discs satisfying \\(f(0)=x\\), the functional \\(J_{h,q,\\delta}\\) has finite supremum, attained by a finite-area holomorphic disc.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(X\\) is a compact connected Kähler manifold of dimension \\(n\\geq 1\\) containing no nonconstant entire curve.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(k\\) is a fixed Kähler metric on \\(X\\), \\(x\\in X\\), \\(h\\) is a smooth Kähler form, \\(q\\) is a smooth closed real \\((1,1)\\)-form, and \\(\\delta&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\mathbb D=\\{z\\in\\mathbb C:|z|&lt;1\\}\\), and let \\(dS\\) be Euclidean area.</p><p class=\"li\">Real \\((1,1)\\)-forms are evaluated through their associated Hermitian tensors. For a holomorphic disc \\(f:\\mathbb D\\to X\\), put\n\\[\nf'(z)=df_z(\\partial_z),\\qquad\nd(f)=k(f'(0),f'(0)).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">A disc has finite area if \\(\\int_{\\mathbb D}k(f',f')\\,dS&lt;\\infty\\). On compact \\(X\\), this condition is independent of the smooth Hermitian metric used.</p><p class=\"li\">For a finite-area disc, define\n\\[\nA_q(f)=\\frac1\\pi\\int_{\\mathbb D}q(f',f')\\,dS,\\qquad\nP_q(f)=\\frac2\\pi\\int_{\\mathbb D}\\log\\frac1{|z|}\\,q(f',f')\\,dS.\n\\]\nThese integrals are finite: away from the center the logarithmic weight is bounded, and near the center the unweighted integrand is smooth.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n\\[\nJ_{h,q,\\delta}(f)=d(f)+P_q(f)-\\delta A_h(f).\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On the finite-area holomorphic discs satisfying \\(f(0)=x\\), the functional \\(J_{h,q,\\delta}\\) has finite supremum, attained by a finite-area holomorphic disc.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(C_q\\geq 0\\) such that\n\\[\n|q(v,v)|\\leq C_qh(v,v)\n\\]\nfor every tangent vector \\(v\\). Select \\(r_0\\in[1/2,1)\\) sufficiently close to \\(1\\) that\n\\[\n2C_q\\log(1/r_0)\\leq \\delta/2.\n\\]\nFor a disc \\(f\\), write\n\\[\na_f(z)=h(f'(z),f'(z)),\\qquad\nb_f(z)=q(f'(z),f'(z)).\n\\]\nOn \\(r_0&lt;|z|&lt;1\\),\n\\[\nT_f(z)=\\delta a_f(z)-2\\log(1/|z|)b_f(z)\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\nT_f(z)\\geq \\frac{\\delta}{2}a_f(z)\\geq 0.\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]</p><p>On \\(|z|&lt;r_0\\), Lemma 1.1 and comparison of \\(h\\) with \\(k\\) give uniform bounds for \\(a_f\\) and \\(|b_f|\\). Integrability of the logarithmic weight gives a constant \\(K\\), independent of \\(f\\), such that\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\frac1\\pi\\int_{|z|&lt;r_0}\n\\bigl(2\\log(1/|z|)b_f-\\delta a_f\\bigr)\\,dS\n\\right|\\leq K.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\nJ_{h,q,\\delta}(f)\n\\leq D+K-\\frac{\\delta}{2\\pi}\n\\int_{r_0&lt;|z|&lt;1}a_f\\,dS.\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]\nThe supremum is finite and is nonnegative because the constant disc has value zero.</p><p>Choose a maximizing sequence \\(f_j\\) with \\(J_{h,q,\\delta}(f_j)\\geq -1\\). Equation (3.4) bounds the outer-annulus areas uniformly, while Lemma 1.1 bounds the inner areas. Thus\n\\[\n\\sup_j A_h(f_j)&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nAfter taking a subsequence, Lemma 1.1 gives locally uniform convergence to a holomorphic disc \\(f\\) centered at \\(x\\), together with derivative convergence on compact subsets. Fatou’s lemma gives\n\\[\nA_h(f)\\leq \\liminf_j A_h(f_j)&lt;\\infty.\n\\]</p><p>The central term converges. The inner-disc integral converges by dominated convergence. On the outer annulus, \\(T_{f_j}\\geq 0\\) converges pointwise to \\(T_f\\), so\n\\[\n-\\frac1\\pi\\int_{r_0&lt;|z|&lt;1}T_f\\,dS\n\\geq\n\\limsup_j\\left(\n-\\frac1\\pi\\int_{r_0&lt;|z|&lt;1}T_{f_j}\\,dS\n\\right).\n\\]\nAll these integrals are finite, by finite area and boundedness of the logarithmic weight on the outer annulus. Combining the terms yields\n\\[\nJ_{h,q,\\delta}(f)\\geq \\limsup_j J_{h,q,\\delta}(f_j),\n\\]\nwhich proves attainment.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1, Derivative bound and normality", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a constant \\(D>0\\), depending only on \\((X,k)\\), such that every holomorphic map \\(f:\\mathbb D\\to X\\) satisfies \\( d(f)=|f'(0)|_k^2\\leq D. \\) Consequently …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(X\\) and \\(k\\) satisfy the assumptions of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">For a holomorphic map \\(f:\\mathbb D\\to X\\), write \\(|f'(z)|_k^2=k(f'(z),f'(z))\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a constant \\(D&gt;0\\), depending only on \\((X,k)\\), such that every holomorphic map \\(f:\\mathbb D\\to X\\) satisfies\n\\[\nd(f)=|f'(0)|_k^2\\leq D.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n|f'(z)|_k\\leq \\frac{\\sqrt D}{1-|z|},\\qquad z\\in\\mathbb D.\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\nEvery sequence of such maps has a subsequence converging uniformly on compact subsets of \\(\\mathbb D\\) to a holomorphic map into \\(X\\). In local target coordinates, all derivatives converge uniformly on smaller compact subsets as well.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose that there are maps \\(f_j:\\mathbb D\\to X\\) with \\(|f_j'(0)|_k\\to\\infty\\). Choose \\(z_j\\) at which\n\\[\nz\\longmapsto (1/2-|z|)|f_j'(z)|_k\n\\]\nattains its maximum on \\(\\{|z|\\leq 1/2\\}\\). For all sufficiently large \\(j\\), this maximum is positive, so \\(|z_j|&lt;1/2\\). Put\n\\[\n\\lambda_j=|f_j'(z_j)|_k,\\qquad\nR_j=(1/2-|z_j|)\\lambda_j,\\qquad\ng_j(w)=f_j(z_j+w/\\lambda_j).\n\\]\nThen \\(g_j\\) is defined on \\(\\{|w|&lt;R_j\\}\\), and\n\\[\nR_j\\geq \\tfrac12|f_j'(0)|_k\\longrightarrow\\infty,\n\\qquad |g_j'(0)|_k=1.\n\\]\nIf \\(|w|&lt;R_j/2\\), then\n\\[\n1/2-|z_j+w/\\lambda_j|\\geq \\tfrac12(1/2-|z_j|).\n\\]\nThe maximizing property of \\(z_j\\) gives \\(|g_j'(w)|_k\\leq 2\\) on this smaller disc.</p><p>For every fixed radius, the maps are equicontinuous and take values in compact \\(X\\). Arzelà–Ascoli and a diagonal subsequence give a locally uniform limit \\(g:\\mathbb C\\to X\\). Near any source point, choose a target chart containing its image under \\(g\\). On a sufficiently small source neighborhood, the limit and all sufficiently late maps lie in that chart. The theorem on uniform limits of holomorphic functions and the Cauchy formula show that \\(g\\) is holomorphic and \\(|g'(0)|_k=1\\), contradicting the hypothesis on entire curves.</p><p>For \\(z\\in\\mathbb D\\), apply the resulting central derivative bound to\n\\[\nw\\longmapsto f(z+(1-|z|)w).\n\\]\nIts central derivative is \\((1-|z|)f'(z)\\), proving (3.1). This gives equicontinuity on every compact source disc. A second application of Arzelà–Ascoli, followed by the same coordinate argument, proves normality and convergence of derivatives. Increase \\(D\\), if necessary, to make it strictly positive.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition 3.4, Boundary regularity of maximizing discs", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The map \\(f\\) extends continuously to \\(\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\) and belongs to \\(W^{1,p}(\\mathbb D,X)\\) for every \\(2<p<4\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(X\\) is a compact connected Kähler manifold containing no nonconstant entire curve; \\(k\\) is a fixed Kähler metric.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(x\\in X\\), a smooth Kähler form \\(h\\), a smooth closed real \\((1,1)\\)-form \\(q\\), and \\(\\delta&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The functionals \\(d,A_q,P_q,J_{h,q,\\delta}\\) have the definitions in Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(0&lt;r&lt;1\\), put\n\\[\nA_{q,r}(f)=\\frac1\\pi\\int_{|z|&lt;r}q(f',f')\\,dS.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">\\(f\\) is a maximizing disc supplied by Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Sobolev regularity of maps and pulled-back bundle sections is understood in finitely many target coordinate charts.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The map \\(f\\) extends continuously to \\(\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\) and belongs to \\(W^{1,p}(\\mathbb D,X)\\) for every \\(2&lt;p&lt;4\\). In particular, it has continuous \\(W^{1,3}\\) regularity up to the boundary. For some finite constant \\(C_f\\),\n\\[\n|f'(z)|_k\\leq C_f(1-|z|)^{-1/2}\n\\tag{3.8}\n\\]\nfor \\(z\\) sufficiently close to \\(\\partial\\mathbb D\\).</p><p>The constants may depend on \\(h,q,\\delta\\), and \\(f\\); the constant \\(D\\) in Lemma 1.1 depends only on \\((X,k)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose finitely many nested coordinate neighborhoods\n\\[\nV_\\alpha\\Subset W_\\alpha\\Subset U_\\alpha\n\\]\nsuch that the \\(V_\\alpha\\) cover \\(X\\) and each \\(U_\\alpha\\) is a holomorphic coordinate chart. There is \\(\\rho&gt;0\\) such that, if \\(y\\in V_\\alpha\\), its metric ball of radius \\(\\rho\\) lies in \\(W_\\alpha\\). On these finitely many \\(W_\\alpha\\), \\(k\\) and the Euclidean coordinate metric are uniformly comparable.</p><p>For \\(z\\in\\mathbb D\\), put \\(t=1-|z|\\). Choose \\(0&lt;c&lt;1/4\\), independently of \\(z\\) and \\(f\\). For \\(w\\in B(z,ct)\\), the segment from \\(z\\) to \\(w\\) stays at source distance at least \\((1-c)t\\) from \\(\\partial\\mathbb D\\). Lemma 1.1 bounds the length of its image by\n\\[\nC\\sqrt D\\,\\frac{c}{1-c},\n\\]\nwhere \\(C\\) accounts for the convention relating real and complex tangent norms. Choose \\(c\\) so that this is less than \\(\\rho\\). If \\(f(z)\\in V_\\alpha\\), then\n\\[\nf(B(z,ct))\\subset W_\\alpha.\n\\]</p><p>In these coordinates, \\(f'\\) is a vector of holomorphic functions, whose squared Euclidean norm is subharmonic. The mean-value inequality and metric comparisons give\n\\[\n|f'(z)|_k^2\n\\leq \\frac{C_1}{t^2}\\int_{B(z,ct)}|f'(w)|_k^2\\,dS(w).\n\\]\nEvery point of \\(B(z,ct)\\) has modulus greater than \\(1-(1+c)t\\). For small \\(t\\), Lemma 2.1 gives\n\\[\n\\int_{B(z,ct)}|f'(w)|_k^2\\,dS(w)\n\\leq\n\\pi\\bigl(A_k(f)-A_{k,1-(1+c)t}(f)\\bigr)\n\\leq C_2t.\n\\]\nThis proves (3.8).</p><p>For \\(r&lt;s&lt;1\\) close to \\(1\\) and \\(\\zeta\\in\\partial\\mathbb D\\), radial integration gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}_k(f(r\\zeta),f(s\\zeta))\n\\leq C_3\\int_r^s(1-u)^{-1/2}\\,du\n\\leq 2C_3\\sqrt{1-r}.\n\\]\nThe estimate is uniform in \\(\\zeta\\). Completeness of \\(X\\) gives a limit along every radial path, and the continuous maps \\(\\zeta\\mapsto f(r\\zeta)\\) converge uniformly to a continuous boundary map. The same estimate proves continuity of the extension on all of \\(\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\).</p><p>For all sufficiently large integers \\(j\\), set\n\\[\nE_j=\\{z\\in\\mathbb D:2^{-j-1}&lt;1-|z|&lt;2^{-j}\\}.\n\\]\nThe area-tail bound and (3.8) imply\n\\[\n\\int_{E_j}|f'|_k^2\\,dS\\leq C_4\\,2^{-j},\n\\qquad\n\\sup_{E_j}|f'|_k\\leq C_5\\,2^{j/2}.\n\\]\nThus, for \\(2&lt;p&lt;4\\),\n\\[\n\\int_{E_j}|f'|_k^p\\,dS\n\\leq\n\\left(\\sup_{E_j}|f'|_k\\right)^{p-2}\n\\int_{E_j}|f'|_k^2\\,dS\n\\leq C_p\\,2^{-j(4-p)/2}.\n\\]\nThis is summable. On the remaining compact interior disc, the derivative is bounded, so \\(|f'|_k\\in L^p(\\mathbb D)\\).</p><p>Continuity on \\(\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\) permits a finite cover of the closed source disc by neighborhoods whose images lie in relatively compact target charts. The coordinate functions are bounded, smooth in the interior, and have first derivatives in \\(L^p\\). Testing against compactly supported smooth functions in the source interior identifies these classical derivatives with weak derivatives. Hence the coordinate functions are \\(W^{1,p}\\) up to the boundary, with the continuous values already constructed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.3, Dilation and area tails", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(0<r<1\\), \\( 0\\leq \\delta\\bigl(A_h(f)-A_{h,r}(f)\\bigr) \\leq (1-r^2)d(f)+2\\int_r^1 A_{q,s}(f)\\,\\frac{ds}{s}. \\) In particular, \\( A_q(f)\\geq -d(f),\\qquad A_k(f)-A_{k,r}(f)\\leq C_f(1-r) \\quad (1/2\\leq r<1) \\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the setting and maximizing disc \\(f\\) of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(0&lt;r&lt;1\\), define \\(f_r(z)=f(rz)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(0&lt;r&lt;1\\),\n\\[\n0\\leq\n\\delta\\bigl(A_h(f)-A_{h,r}(f)\\bigr)\n\\leq\n(1-r^2)d(f)+2\\int_r^1 A_{q,s}(f)\\,\\frac{ds}{s}.\n\\tag{3.5}\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\nA_q(f)\\geq -d(f),\\qquad\nA_k(f)-A_{k,r}(f)\\leq C_f(1-r)\n\\quad (1/2\\leq r&lt;1)\n\\tag{3.6}\n\\]\nfor some finite constant \\(C_f\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>A change of variables gives\n\\[\nd(f_r)=r^2d(f),\\qquad\nA_h(f_r)=A_{h,r}(f),\\qquad\nA_{q,s}(f_r)=A_{q,rs}(f).\n\\]\nUsing\n\\[\n\\log(1/|z|)=\\int_{|z|}^1\\frac{ds}{s}\n\\]\nand Fubini’s theorem,\n\\[\nP_q(f)=2\\int_0^1A_{q,s}(f)\\,\\frac{ds}{s}.\n\\]\nFubini is valid for the signed integrand: derivatives are bounded near the origin, and away from the origin the logarithmic weight is bounded while \\(|q(f',f')|\\) is controlled by finite \\(h\\)-area. Applying the same identity to \\(f_r\\) gives\n\\[\nP_q(f)-P_q(f_r)=2\\int_r^1A_{q,s}(f)\\,\\frac{ds}{s}.\n\\tag{3.7}\n\\]\nSince \\(f_r\\) is admissible, maximality gives \\(J_{h,q,\\delta}(f)\\geq J_{h,q,\\delta}(f_r)\\). Substitution proves (3.5), whose leftmost inequality follows from positivity of \\(h\\).</p><p>Divide its rightmost expression by \\(1-r\\). As \\(r\\uparrow1\\), it tends to \\(2d(f)+2A_q(f)\\). Nonnegativity gives \\(A_q(f)\\geq-d(f)\\). For \\(r\\geq1/2\\), the right-hand side of (3.5) is at most\n\\[\n2d(f)(1-r)+4C_qA_h(f)(1-r).\n\\]\nThis bounds the \\(h\\)-area tail by a constant times \\(1-r\\). Uniform comparison of \\(h\\) and \\(k\\) proves (3.6).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition 4.2, Boundary-unitary frame", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There exist \\(p_0\\) with \\(2<p_0\\leq p\\) and a holomorphic frame \\( B=(b_1,\\ldots,b_n) \\) of \\(f^*T^{1,0}X\\), continuous, nonsingular, and \\(W^{1,p_0}\\) on \\(\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\), such that …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(X\\) is a compact Kähler manifold. Unlike Propositions 1–2, the absence of nonconstant entire curves is not required for this construction.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(f:\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\to X\\) is continuous, holomorphic on \\(\\mathbb D\\), and of class \\(W^{1,p}\\) for some \\(p&gt;2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(h\\) is a smooth Hermitian metric on \\(X\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Vectors are columns; the Gram matrix of a frame \\(B\\) is \\(B^*hB\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exist \\(p_0\\) with \\(2&lt;p_0\\leq p\\) and a holomorphic frame\n\\[\nB=(b_1,\\ldots,b_n)\n\\]\nof \\(f^*T^{1,0}X\\), continuous, nonsingular, and \\(W^{1,p_0}\\) on \\(\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\), such that\n\\[\nB(z)^*h(f(z))B(z)=I,\\qquad z\\in\\partial\\mathbb D.\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(e\\) by Lemma 3.1 and put\n\\[\nM(z)=e(z)^*h(f(z))e(z).\n\\]\nThis is a continuous positive-definite \\(W^{1,p}\\) matrix on \\(\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\). There are constants \\(0&lt;m\\leq M_0&lt;\\infty\\) with\n\\[\nmI\\leq M(z)\\leq M_0I.\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]</p><p>For the Hardy-space factorization, let \\(H^2=H^2(\\partial\\mathbb D,\\mathbb C^n)\\) be the vector Hardy space of nonnegative Fourier modes, and set \\(d\\sigma=d\\theta/(2\\pi)\\). Equip it with the equivalent norm\n\\[\n\\|u\\|_M^2=\\int_{\\partial\\mathbb D}u^*Mu\\,d\\sigma.\n\\]\nMultiplication by \\(z\\) is an isometry, and \\(zH^2\\) has codimension \\(n\\). Choose an orthonormal basis \\(s_1,\\ldots,s_n\\) of\n\\[\nW=H^2\\ominus_M zH^2,\n\\]\nand form \\(S=(s_1,\\ldots,s_n)\\). The vectors \\(z^js_a\\), \\(j\\geq0\\), \\(1\\leq a\\leq n\\), form an orthonormal basis. Indeed, iterating\n\\[\nH^2=W\\oplus_M zH^2\n\\]\nshows that the orthogonal complement of their span lies in every \\(z^jH^2\\), whose intersection is zero.</p><p>Their orthogonality identifies all Fourier coefficients of \\(S^*MS\\), giving\n\\[\nS^*MS=I\\quad\\text{almost everywhere on }\\partial\\mathbb D.\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]\nEquation (4.4) bounds the boundary values of \\(S\\) in \\(L^\\infty\\). The holomorphic Hardy extension is bounded in \\(\\mathbb D\\), entrywise by the Poisson integral. It is invertible at every \\(\\zeta\\in\\mathbb D\\): evaluation at \\(\\zeta\\) is a continuous surjection \\(H^2\\to\\mathbb C^n\\), whereas the evaluations of all \\(z^js_a\\) lie in the range of \\(S(\\zeta)\\); density forces that range to be \\(\\mathbb C^n\\).</p><p>To obtain boundary regularity, write\n\\[\nM_\\theta(e^{i\\alpha})=M(e^{i(\\alpha+\\theta)}).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\|M_\\theta-M\\|_{L^p(\\partial\\mathbb D)}\n\\leq C|\\theta|^{1-1/p},\\qquad |\\theta|\\leq\\tfrac14.\n\\tag{4.6}\n\\]\nFor \\(t=|\\theta|\\), choose \\(\\rho\\in[1-2t,1-t]\\) such that the angular \\(L^p\\) norm of \\(\\partial_\\alpha M(\\rho e^{i\\alpha})\\) is at most \\(Ct^{-1/p}\\|M\\|_{W^{1,p}}\\). Radial integration and Hölder’s inequality bound\n\\[\n\\|M|_{\\partial\\mathbb D}-M(\\rho\\,\\cdot)\\|_{L^p}\n\\]\nby \\(Ct^{1-1/p}\\|M\\|_{W^{1,p}}\\). Angular integration at radius \\(\\rho\\) gives the same bound for translation by \\(\\theta\\). The triangle inequality proves (4.6); approximation proves it for Sobolev functions, with continuity identifying the boundary trace.</p><p>Let \\(H^s(S^1)\\) denote the fractional Sobolev space defined by Fourier weight \\(1+|\\ell|^{2s}\\), distinct from the Hardy notation \\(H^2\\). Equation (4.6) implies\n\\[\nM|_{\\partial\\mathbb D}\\in H^s(S^1)\n\\quad\\text{for every}\\quad\n\\frac12&lt;s&lt;1-\\frac1p.\n\\tag{4.7}\n\\]\nIndeed, the \\(L^p\\) bound controls the \\(L^2\\) bound, and integration of squared rotation differences against \\(|\\theta|^{-1-2s}\\,d\\theta\\) is finite. Parseval identifies this with the Fourier definition.</p><p>Let \\(\\Pi\\) be the unweighted Hardy projection. The Toeplitz operator\n\\[\nT_Mv=\\Pi(Mv),\\qquad T_M:H^2\\to H^2\n\\]\nis bounded, self-adjoint, and satisfies\n\\[\n\\langle T_Mv,v\\rangle\\geq m\\|v\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nIt has bounded inverse: its range is closed by the lower bound and dense because its orthogonal complement is the kernel of its adjoint. For a column \\(v\\) of \\(S\\), membership in \\(W\\) says that \\(T_Mv\\) is constant. Rotating and subtracting gives\n\\[\nT_M(v_\\theta-v)=\\Pi((M-M_\\theta)v_\\theta).\n\\]\nBoundedness of \\(v\\) and \\(T_M^{-1}\\) yields\n\\[\n\\|v_\\theta-v\\|_2\n\\leq C\\|(M_\\theta-M)v_\\theta\\|_2\n\\leq C\\|M_\\theta-M\\|_2.\n\\tag{4.8}\n\\]\nThus \\(v\\in H^s\\).</p><p>Write \\(v(z)=\\sum_{\\ell\\geq0}v_\\ell z^\\ell\\). Since \\(s&gt;1/2\\), Cauchy–Schwarz gives absolute uniform convergence on \\(\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\), hence continuity. For \\(1/2&lt;r&lt;1\\),\n\\[\n\\|v'(r\\,\\cdot)\\|_2^2\n\\leq C(1-r)^{2s-2}\n\\sum_{\\ell\\geq1}\\ell^{2s}|v_\\ell|^2,\n\\tag{4.9}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\|v'(r\\,\\cdot)\\|_\\infty\n\\leq C(1-r)^{s-3/2}\n\\left(\\sum_{\\ell\\geq1}\\ell^{2s}|v_\\ell|^2\\right)^{1/2}.\n\\tag{4.10}\n\\]\nInterpolating bounds \\(\\|v'(r\\,\\cdot)\\|_{p_0}^{p_0}\\) by a constant times\n\\[\n(1-r)^{(s-3/2)(p_0-2)+2s-2}\n=(1-r)^{p_0(s-3/2)+1}.\n\\]\nThis is integrable if\n\\[\n2&lt;p_0&lt;\\frac2{3/2-s}.\n\\tag{4.11}\n\\]\nThe interval is nonempty because \\(s&gt;1/2\\); choose also \\(p_0\\leq p\\). Hence \\(S\\in W^{1,p_0}(\\mathbb D)\\).</p><p>By continuity, (4.5) holds at every boundary point, so \\(S\\) is invertible there as well as in the interior. Compactness gives uniform nonsingularity. Finally \\(B=eS\\) is the required frame. Uniform nonsingularity was obtained after boundary regularity, not assumed in the Hardy-space argument.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The bundle \\(f^*T^{1,0}X\\) admits a holomorphic frame \\(e\\) on \\(\\mathbb D\\) extending to a continuous, nonsingular \\(W^{1,p}\\) frame on \\(\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(X,f,p\\) satisfy the assumptions of Proposition 3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The bundle \\(f^*T^{1,0}X\\) admits a holomorphic frame \\(e\\) on \\(\\mathbb D\\) extending to a continuous, nonsingular \\(W^{1,p}\\) frame on \\(\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The continuous pullback bundle over \\(\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\) is topologically trivial. Its coordinate transition matrices are \\(W^{1,p}\\), being smooth functions of \\(f\\) with bounded derivatives on the compact target subsets in use. Start with a continuous frame, approximate its coefficients uniformly in finitely many local frames, and combine the approximations by a smooth partition of unity on the source. This gives a \\(W^{1,p}\\) frame \\(v\\) uniformly close to the original one; sufficiently close approximation preserves pointwise independence.</p><p>In the frame \\(v\\), the Dolbeault operator has the form\n\\[\n\\bar\\partial+a,\\qquad a\\in L^p(\\mathbb D,\\operatorname{Mat}_n(\\mathbb C)),\n\\]\nwhere a \\((0,1)\\)-form is identified with its coefficient. Extend \\(a\\) by zero to \\(\\mathbb D_R=\\{|z|&lt;R\\}\\), \\(R&gt;1\\). On a sufficiently small disc \\(U\\subset\\mathbb D_R\\) of radius \\(r\\), solve\n\\[\n\\bar\\partial U_0=-aU_0.\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nThe solid Cauchy transform\n\\[\nT_Ug(z)=\\frac1\\pi\\int_U\\frac{g(\\zeta)}{z-\\zeta}\\,dS(\\zeta)\n\\]\nsatisfies \\(\\bar\\partial T_Ug=g\\) weakly. Hölder’s inequality and the \\(L^p\\) estimate for the planar Cauchy singular integral, cited in [28, Section 4.1 of the authors’ preprint], give\n\\[\n\\|T_Ug\\|_{L^\\infty(U)}\n\\leq C_pr^{1-2/p}\\|g\\|_{L^p(U)},\n\\qquad\n\\|T_Ug\\|_{W^{1,p}(U)}\n\\leq C_{p,r}\\|g\\|_{L^p(U)}.\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\nFor small \\(r\\), the equation \\(U_0=I-T_U(aU_0)\\) is solved by contraction in the sup norm. Choose the radius so that \\(U_0\\) stays within distance \\(1/2\\) of \\(I\\); it is invertible and \\(W^{1,p}\\).</p><p>For two such solutions \\(U_i,U_j\\), the matrix \\(U_i^{-1}U_j\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\bar\\partial(U_i^{-1}U_j)=0\n\\]\nweakly on the overlap, hence is holomorphic. These transitions define a holomorphic vector bundle on \\(\\mathbb D_R\\). By the Oka–Grauert principle [15, 12], this bundle is holomorphically trivial. Choose a global holomorphic frame. Relative to the local frames \\(U_i\\), its coefficients are holomorphic; relative to the original trivial bundle they are \\(W^{1,p}\\) on compact subsets of \\(\\mathbb D_R\\). Thus the resulting matrix \\(U\\) is continuous, invertible, and \\(W^{1,p}\\) on \\(\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\), and satisfies \\(\\bar\\partial U=-aU\\) on \\(\\mathbb D\\). The frame \\(e=vU\\) has the required properties.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Proposition 4.3, Centered holomorphic variations", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For some \\(\\eta>0\\), there is a continuous map \\( F:\\{t\\in\\mathbb C^n:|t|<\\eta\\}\\times\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\to X, \\) jointly holomorphic on \\(\\{|t|<\\eta\\}\\times\\mathbb D\\), satisfying …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(X\\) is a compact Kähler manifold; no hyperbolicity assumption is required here.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(f:\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\to X\\) is continuous, holomorphic on \\(\\mathbb D\\), and \\(W^{1,p}\\) for some \\(p&gt;2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(h\\) is a smooth Hermitian metric.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(B=(b_1,\\ldots,b_n)\\) and \\(2&lt;p_0\\leq p\\) are supplied by Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Set \\(x=f(0)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For some \\(\\eta&gt;0\\), there is a continuous map\n\\[\nF:\\{t\\in\\mathbb C^n:|t|&lt;\\eta\\}\\times\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\to X,\n\\]\njointly holomorphic on \\(\\{|t|&lt;\\eta\\}\\times\\mathbb D\\), satisfying\n\\[\nF(0,z)=f(z),\\qquad F(t,0)=x,\\qquad\n\\frac{\\partial F}{\\partial t_j}(0,z)=zb_j(z).\n\\tag{4.12}\n\\]\nIn fixed local target coordinates covering the graph of \\(f\\), the maps \\(t\\mapsto F(t,\\cdot)\\) are holomorphic with values in \\(W^{1,p_0}\\) on the corresponding source patches. Every \\(F(t,\\cdot)\\) is therefore a finite-area holomorphic disc centered at \\(x\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose the frame \\(e\\) constructed in the proof of Proposition 3 and write \\(B=eS\\). Then \\(S=e^{-1}B\\) is holomorphic on \\(\\mathbb D\\) and \\(W^{1,p_0}\\) on \\(\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\).</p><p>First construct fiber coordinates near the graph. Choose a finite relatively open cover \\(U_i\\) of \\(\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\) and target charts \\(\\varphi_i:V_i\\to\\mathbb C^n\\) such that \\(f(\\overline U_i)\\) is compact in \\(V_i\\), after shrinking source sets as necessary. Let \\(\\rho_i\\) be a smooth partition of unity subordinate to this cover. Put\n\\[\nA_i(z)=D\\varphi_i(f(z))e(z),\\qquad\nC_i(z,y)=A_i(z)^{-1}\\bigl(\\varphi_i(y)-\\varphi_i(f(z))\\bigr).\n\\]\nOn \\(U_i\\), \\(A_i\\) is holomorphic in the interior, continuous, invertible, and \\(W^{1,p_0}\\) up to the boundary. The map \\(C_i\\) is holomorphic in \\(y\\) and, for fixed admissible \\(y\\), in the interior variable \\(z\\). Define\n\\[\n\\Phi(z,y)=\\sum_i\\rho_i(z)C_i(z,y)\n\\tag{4.13}\n\\]\nnear the graph. Each term is used only where its chart is defined; compact support inside the source patch makes extension by zero harmless. On sufficiently small source patches, all charts whose supports meet the patch are defined on a common target neighborhood of its image. Finiteness and subordination give uniform neighborhoods. By construction,\n\\[\n\\Phi(z,f(z))=0,\\qquad D_y\\Phi(z,f(z))e(z)=I.\n\\tag{4.14}\n\\]</p><p>For uniform inverses, work in one such source patch with target chart \\(\\psi\\), and set\n\\[\nc(z)=\\psi(f(z)),\\qquad e_\\psi(z)=D\\psi(f(z))e(z),\n\\]\n\\[\nK_z(v)=\\Phi\\bigl(z,\\psi^{-1}(c(z)+e_\\psi(z)v)\\bigr).\n\\]\nThen \\(K_z(0)=0\\) and \\(D_vK_z(0)=I\\). Continuity of fiber derivatives and compactness give a common \\(R&gt;0\\) such that the maps are defined on \\(|v|\\leq R\\) and\n\\[\n\\|D_vK_z(v)-I\\|\\leq\\tfrac14.\n\\]\nFor \\(|\\xi|&lt;R/2\\),\n\\[\nv=\\xi-(K_z(v)-v)\n\\]\nis a contraction of the closed radius-\\(R\\) ball into itself. Its solution \\(V(z,\\xi)\\) is continuous in \\((z,\\xi)\\) and holomorphic in \\(\\xi\\), by uniformly convergent contraction iterations. Define \\(E\\) by\n\\[\n\\psi(E(z,\\xi))=c(z)+e_\\psi(z)V(z,\\xi).\n\\]\nLocal inverses agree near \\(\\xi=0\\) by uniqueness and throughout common fiber domains by holomorphy. Taking the minimum of finitely many radii gives\n\\[\n\\Phi(z,E(z,\\xi))=\\xi,\\qquad\nE(z,0)=f(z),\\qquad E_\\xi(z,0)=e(z).\n\\tag{4.15}\n\\]</p><p>The chart formula for \\(K_z\\) depends smoothly on the finite coefficient list\n\\[\na(z)=\\bigl(\\rho_i(z),A_i(z)^{-1},\n\\varphi_i(f(z)),c(z),e_\\psi(z)\\bigr)_i.\n\\]\nThese coefficients are \\(W^{1,p_0}\\) with compact range. On a smaller closed fiber polydisc, the strict contraction bounds persist for coefficient lists in a small neighborhood of this compact range. The finite-dimensional implicit function theorem, treating the coefficients as real parameters, makes \\(V\\) smooth in that list and \\(\\xi\\), and holomorphic in \\(\\xi\\). Its first coefficient derivatives are uniformly bounded after shrinking the neighborhood. The same holds for\n\\[\nE_\\psi(z,\\xi)=\\psi(E(z,\\xi)).\n\\]\nThe Sobolev chain rule gives a single \\(b\\in L^{p_0}\\) on the source patch such that\n\\[\n|E_\\psi(z,\\xi)|\\leq C,\\qquad\n|\\nabla_zE_\\psi(z,\\xi)|\\leq Cb(z)\n\\tag{4.16}\n\\]\nalmost everywhere, uniformly on the smaller fiber polydisc. One may take \\(b\\) to be a constant plus the sum of absolute values of the weak first derivatives of the coefficient list. Cauchy’s formula gives the same type of bound for fiber derivatives after another fixed shrink.</p><p>These estimates justify holomorphy of substitution\n\\[\nu\\longmapsto E_\\psi(\\cdot,u(\\cdot))\n\\tag{4.17}\n\\]\nfrom a neighborhood of zero in \\(W^{1,p_0}(\\mathbb D,\\mathbb C^n)\\) into the local \\(W^{1,p_0}\\) space. Expand\n\\[\nE_\\psi(z,\\xi)=\\sum_{\\alpha\\in\\mathbb N^n}E_\\alpha(z)\\xi^\\alpha.\n\\]\nCauchy’s formula, with weak \\(z\\)-differentiation under its contour integral, gives\n\\[\n\\|E_\\alpha\\|_\\infty+\\|\\nabla E_\\alpha\\|_{p_0}\n\\leq Cr_1^{-|\\alpha|}\n\\]\nfor some \\(r_1&gt;0\\). The algebra norm \\(\\|a\\|_\\infty+\\|\\nabla a\\|_{p_0}\\) is submultiplicative. Thus \\(\\sum_\\alpha E_\\alpha u^\\alpha\\) converges normally in local \\(W^{1,p_0}\\) when the global \\(W^{1,p_0}\\) norm of \\(u\\) is small; the number of multi-indices of each degree grows polynomially. Each term is a continuous homogeneous polynomial. This uses only fixed local target coordinates, not a linear structure on \\(X\\).</p><p>For \\(u\\) close to zero, the Sobolev chain rule gives\n\\[\n\\bar\\partial(E(z,u(z)))\n=\nE_\\xi(z,u(z))\\bar\\partial u+\\bar\\partial_zE(z,u(z)).\n\\]\nThere is no \\(\\overline{\\bar\\partial u}\\) term because \\(E\\) is holomorphic in \\(\\xi\\). The map is weakly holomorphic precisely when\n\\[\n\\bar\\partial u+Q(\\cdot,u)=0,\\qquad\nQ(z,\\xi)=E_\\xi(z,\\xi)^{-1}\\bar\\partial_zE(z,\\xi).\n\\tag{4.18}\n\\]\nThis is independent of target chart, since a holomorphic chart change multiplies both terms by its derivative.</p><p>Differentiate \\(\\Phi(z,E(z,\\xi))=\\xi\\) with \\(\\xi\\) fixed, using \\(E_\\xi=(D_y\\Phi)^{-1}\\):\n\\[\nQ(z,\\xi)\n=-\\bar\\partial_z\\Phi(z,E(z,\\xi))\n=-\\sum_i(\\bar\\partial\\rho_i)(z)C_i(z,E(z,\\xi)).\n\\tag{4.19}\n\\]\nThe second equality uses holomorphy of \\(C_i\\) in \\(z\\) with \\(y\\) fixed. Thus \\(Q\\) is holomorphic in \\(\\xi\\), jointly continuous, and bounded on the closed disc times a smaller fiber polydisc. Its Taylor coefficients satisfy\n\\[\n\\|Q_\\alpha\\|_\\infty\\leq Cr_2^{-|\\alpha|}\n\\]\nfor some \\(r_2&gt;0\\). The normally convergent series \\(\\sum_\\alpha Q_\\alpha u^\\alpha\\), using \\(W^{1,p_0}\\hookrightarrow C^0\\), proves that\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q:W^{1,p_0}(\\mathbb D,\\mathbb C^n)\\to\nL^{p_0}(\\mathbb D,\\mathbb C^n),\\qquad\n\\mathcal Q(u)=Q(\\cdot,u(\\cdot)),\n\\tag{4.20}\n\\]\nis holomorphic near zero.</p><p>Since\n\\[\nC_i(z,f(z))=0,\\qquad\nD_yC_i(z,f(z))e(z)=I,\\qquad\n\\sum_i\\bar\\partial\\rho_i=0,\n\\]\nwe obtain\n\\[\nQ(z,0)=0,\\qquad Q_\\xi(z,0)=0,\n\\tag{4.21}\n\\]\nequivalently \\(\\mathcal Q(0)=0\\) and \\(D\\mathcal Q(0)=0\\).</p><p>Let \\(T:L^{p_0}\\to W^{1,p_0}\\) be the solid Cauchy right inverse on the full disc. Point evaluation is bounded on \\(W^{1,p_0}\\), so\n\\[\nHg=Tg-(Tg)(0)\n\\]\nis bounded and satisfies\n\\[\n\\bar\\partial Hg=g,\\qquad (Hg)(0)=0.\n\\tag{4.22}\n\\]\nSeek\n\\[\nu(t,z)=zS(z)t+Hg(t)(z),\\qquad\ng(t)\\in L^{p_0}(\\mathbb D,\\mathbb C^n).\n\\tag{4.23}\n\\]\nEquation (4.18) becomes\n\\[\nG(g,t):=g+\\mathcal Q(zSt+Hg)=0.\n\\]\nIt is holomorphic with values in \\(L^{p_0}\\), with\n\\[\nG(0,0)=0,\\qquad D_gG(0,0)=I.\n\\]\nThe complex Banach implicit function theorem [14, Theorem 2.3(ii), (d) of the author’s preprint] gives a holomorphic solution \\(g(t)\\) near zero. Differentiation yields\n\\[\ng(0)=0,\\qquad D_tg(0)=0.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nu(t,0)=0,\\qquad u(0,z)=0,\\qquad\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial t_j}(0,z)=zS(z)e_j,\n\\]\nwhere \\(e_j\\) is the \\(j\\)-th standard vector of \\(\\mathbb C^n\\).</p><p>Set \\(F(t,z)=E(z,u(t,z))\\). Equations (4.15) and \\(eS=B\\) give (4.12). Holomorphic substitution gives the claimed local \\(W^{1,p_0}\\) parameter dependence and joint continuity up to the boundary. For fixed \\(t\\), (4.18) makes the coordinate functions weakly, hence ordinarily, holomorphic in the open disc. For fixed \\(z\\), bounded point evaluation and fiber holomorphy give holomorphy in \\(t\\). Local separate holomorphy gives joint holomorphy.</p><p>Finally \\(F(t,\\cdot)\\) is \\(W^{1,p_0}\\), \\(p_0&gt;2\\), with compact image, hence has finite area for every smooth Hermitian metric. All small parameters therefore give admissible centered discs. No condition is imposed on their boundary maps, and no extension across \\(\\partial\\mathbb D\\) is required.</p><p>As noted in the paper’s Remark 4.4, derivative evaluation at a point is bounded on the holomorphic subspace on each smaller disc by the Cauchy formula. In particular, \\(t\\mapsto\\partial_zF(t,0)\\) is holomorphic.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Lemma 5.1 and Proposition 5.2, Determinant and trace identities", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The determinant identity is \\( \\log\\det N(0)=P_{R_h}(f). \\) The trace identities are \\( \\mathcal Ld(F_t)=\\operatorname{tr}(B(0)^*k(x)B(0)), \\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(X\\) is a compact Kähler manifold with a fixed Kähler metric \\(k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(f:\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\to X\\) is continuous, holomorphic on \\(\\mathbb D\\), and \\(W^{1,p}\\) for some \\(p&gt;2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(h\\) is a smooth Kähler metric, and \\(q\\) is a smooth closed real \\((1,1)\\)-form.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(B=(b_1,\\ldots,b_n)\\) is a boundary-unitary frame supplied by Proposition 3, and \\(F\\) is its centered holomorphic variation supplied by Proposition 4. Put \\(x=f(0)\\) and \\(F_t(z)=F(t,z)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Decrease the Sobolev exponent if necessary so that maps and frames have \\(W^{1,p_0}\\) regularity for one \\(p_0&gt;2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use\n\\[\ndd^c=i\\partial\\bar\\partial,\\qquad\nR_h=-i\\partial\\bar\\partial\\log\\det h.\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nThus \\([R_h]=2\\pi c_1(T^{1,0}X)\\) and \\([-R_h]=2\\pi c_1(K_X)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(d,A_q,P_q\\) as in Proposition 1, and put\n\\[\nN(z)=B(z)^*h(f(z))B(z),\\qquad\n\\mathcal L=\\left.\\sum_{j=1}^n\n\\frac{\\partial^2}{\\partial t_j\\partial\\overline t_j}\\right|_{t=0}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a circle function \\(u\\), write\n\\[\n\\langle u\\rangle_r=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi}u(re^{i\\theta})\\,d\\theta.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The determinant identity is\n\\[\n\\log\\det N(0)=P_{R_h}(f).\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\nThe trace identities are\n\\[\n\\mathcal Ld(F_t)=\\operatorname{tr}(B(0)^*k(x)B(0)),\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal LP_q(F_t)\n=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\n(\\operatorname{tr}_h q)(f(e^{i\\theta}))\\,d\\theta,\n\\tag{5.3}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal LA_h(F_t)=n-A_{R_h}(f).\n\\tag{5.4}\n\\]\nHere \\(F_t\\) denotes a disc, not a parameter derivative. No extension of \\(f\\) across the boundary is assumed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 5.2 proves the determinant identity.</p><p>Since \\(F(t,0)=x\\), the vector \\(\\partial_zF(t,0)\\) is holomorphic in \\(t\\) and lies in the fixed vector space \\(T_x^{1,0}X\\). Its \\(t_j\\)-derivative at zero is \\(b_j(0)\\). Differentiating its squared \\(k(x)\\)-norm proves (5.2). Its holomorphic parameter dependence also follows directly from the Cauchy formula on a smaller source circle.</p><p>To justify differentiation under the integrals, choose finitely many fixed source patches and target charts containing the image of \\(F\\) for all sufficiently small \\(t\\). In each chart, \\(t\\mapsto F(t,\\cdot)\\) is holomorphic into \\(W^{1,p_0}\\). Smooth coefficient composition and the product rule show that\n\\[\nt\\longmapsto q(F_z(t,\\cdot),F_z(t,\\cdot))\n\\]\nis twice continuously differentiable into \\(L^{p_0/2}\\); the same holds with \\(h\\). Each parameter derivative is a sum of products of two first source derivatives in \\(L^{p_0}\\), with uniformly bounded Sobolev coefficient factors. A fixed source partition of unity gives the global assertion. Since \\(p_0/2&gt;1\\), integration against \\(1\\) or \\(\\log(1/|z|)\\) is continuous on this space.</p><p>Locally write \\(q=i\\partial\\bar\\partial\\varphi\\). Joint holomorphy gives\n\\[\nq(F_z,F_z)=\\partial_z\\partial_{\\bar z}(\\varphi\\circ F),\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal L(\\varphi\\circ F)\n=\\left.\\sum_jq(F_{t_j},F_{t_j})\\right|_{t=0}.\n\\]\nCommuting derivatives and using \\(F_{t_j}(0,z)=zb_j(z)\\) yields\n\\[\n\\mathcal Lq(F_z,F_z)\n=\n\\partial_z\\partial_{\\bar z}\n\\left(|z|^2\\operatorname{tr}(B^*q(f)B)\\right).\n\\tag{5.5}\n\\]\nThe mixed derivative belongs to \\(L^{p_0/2}\\) by the preceding differentiation argument. The scalar function in parentheses is continuous on the closed disc and vanishes at zero. Lemma 5.1 computes its Poisson integral from its boundary values. Since the boundary columns of \\(B\\) are \\(h\\)-orthonormal,\n\\(\\operatorname{tr}(B^*qB)=\\operatorname{tr}_h q\\) there. This proves (5.3).</p><p>For the area identity set \\(T=\\operatorname{tr}N\\). Integrating (5.5) with \\(q=h\\) on \\(|z|&lt;r\\) gives\n\\[\n\\frac1\\pi\\int_{|z|&lt;r}\\mathcal Lh(F_z,F_z)\\,dS\n=\nr^2\\langle T\\rangle_r+\\frac{r^3}{2}\\langle T_r\\rangle_r.\n\\tag{5.6}\n\\]\nThe left-hand side tends to \\(\\mathcal LA_h(F_t)\\), and the first term on the right tends to \\(n\\).</p><p>At the identity matrix, trace and log determinant have the same differential. More explicitly,\n\\[\nT_r-(\\log\\det N)_r\n=\\operatorname{tr}((I-N^{-1})N_r).\n\\tag{5.7}\n\\]\nUniform positive definiteness gives\n\\[\n\\left|\\langle T_r-(\\log\\det N)_r\\rangle_r\\right|\n\\leq C\\|N(r,\\cdot)-I\\|_{L^2(S^1)}\n       \\|N_r(r,\\cdot)\\|_{L^2(S^1)}.\n\\]\nThe radial \\(W^{1,2}\\) estimate and boundary trace \\(N=I\\) imply\n\\[\n\\|N(r,\\cdot)-I\\|_2\n\\leq\n(1-r)^{1/2}\n\\left(\\int_r^1\\|N_s(s,\\cdot)\\|_2^2\\,ds\\right)^{1/2}\n=o((1-r)^{1/2}).\n\\tag{5.8}\n\\]\nThis follows from the fundamental theorem for Sobolev functions on almost every radial line, then Cauchy–Schwarz and angular integration. Interior smoothness and boundary continuity give the same estimate for the interior radii used below.</p><p>Since\n\\[\n\\int_{1/2}^1\\|N_r(r,\\cdot)\\|_2^2\\,dr&lt;\\infty,\n\\]\nthere are radii \\(r_\\nu\\uparrow1\\) for which\n\\((1-r_\\nu)\\|N_r(r_\\nu,\\cdot)\\|_2^2\\) is bounded; otherwise an eventual lower bound would contradict integrability. Along these radii, (5.7)–(5.8) show that the two circular derivative means differ by \\(o(1)\\).</p><p>The curvature computation in Lemma 5.2 and (2.4) give\n\\[\n\\frac r2\\langle(\\log\\det N)_r\\rangle_r\n=-A_{R_h,r}(f),\n\\]\nwhose right-hand side tends to \\(-A_{R_h}(f)\\). Substitute into (5.6) along the selected radii. Its left-hand side has a limit independent of the sequence, proving (5.4).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1, Green identities", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(0<r<1\\), \\( \\langle u\\rangle_r-u(0) =\\frac2\\pi\\int_{|z|<r}\\log\\frac r{|z|} \\,\\partial_z\\partial_{\\bar z}u\\,dS, \\) \\( \\frac1\\pi\\int_{|z|<r}\\partial_z\\partial_{\\bar z}u\\,dS =\\frac r2\\langle\\partial_ru\\rangle_r. \\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(u\\) is a smooth scalar function on \\(\\mathbb D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(dS\\) is Euclidean area and \\(\\langle u\\rangle_r\\) is the normalized circle mean.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(0&lt;r&lt;1\\),\n\\[\n\\langle u\\rangle_r-u(0)\n=\\frac2\\pi\\int_{|z|&lt;r}\\log\\frac r{|z|}\n\\,\\partial_z\\partial_{\\bar z}u\\,dS,\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac1\\pi\\int_{|z|&lt;r}\\partial_z\\partial_{\\bar z}u\\,dS\n=\\frac r2\\langle\\partial_ru\\rangle_r.\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\nIf additionally \\(u\\) is continuous on \\(\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\) and\n\\(\\partial_z\\partial_{\\bar z}u\\in L^a(\\mathbb D)\\) for some \\(a&gt;1\\), then (2.3) passes to \\(r=1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>These are the ordinary planar Green identities with\n\\(\\Delta=4\\partial_z\\partial_{\\bar z}\\). Since \\(\\log(1/|z|)\\) belongs to every finite \\(L^b(\\mathbb D)\\), Hölder’s inequality and dominated convergence give the limit on the right-hand side of (2.3). Continuity gives convergence of the circle means.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.1, Determinant identity", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( \\log\\det N(0)=P_{R_h}(f). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(f,h,B,N,p_0\\) from Proposition 5.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\log\\det N(0)=P_{R_h}(f).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>In a holomorphic target chart, \\(B\\) is holomorphic and nonsingular, so\n\\[\n\\partial_z\\partial_{\\bar z}\\log\\det N\n=\n\\partial_z\\partial_{\\bar z}\\log\\det h(f(z))\n=\n-R_h(f',f').\n\\]\nThe first equality follows because \\(\\log|\\det B|^2\\) is harmonic; the formula is independent of target chart. The right-hand side belongs to \\(L^{p_0/2}\\). Apply Lemma 5.1, using \\(\\log\\det N=0\\) on the boundary.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Corollary 5.3, Maximizing inequality, together with its recorded determinant and dilation consequences", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\operatorname{tr}(B(0)^*k(x)B(0)) +\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi} (\\operatorname{tr}_h q)(f(e^{i\\theta}))\\,d\\theta \\leq \\delta\\bigl(n-A_{R_h}(f)\\bigr). \\) Moreover …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(X\\) is a compact connected Kähler manifold containing no nonconstant entire curve; \\(k\\) is fixed.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(x\\in X\\), a smooth Kähler form \\(h\\), a smooth closed real \\((1,1)\\)-form \\(q\\), and \\(\\delta&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f\\) maximize \\(d+P_q-\\delta A_h\\) among finite-area discs centered at \\(x\\), as in Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B\\) be its boundary-unitary frame from Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">The functionals and curvature \\(R_h=-i\\partial\\bar\\partial\\log\\det h\\) have the definitions above.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(B(0)^*k(x)B(0))\n+\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\n(\\operatorname{tr}_h q)(f(e^{i\\theta}))\\,d\\theta\n\\leq\n\\delta\\bigl(n-A_{R_h}(f)\\bigr).\n\\tag{5.9}\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\log\\det(B(0)^*h(x)B(0))=P_{R_h}(f),\n\\qquad\nA_q(f)\\geq-d(f).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 2 gives the boundary regularity needed for Propositions 3–4. Every sufficiently small parameter in the resulting centered family gives an admissible finite-area disc. Hence the twice differentiable real-valued function \\(J_{h,q,\\delta}(F_t)\\) has a local maximum at zero, so\n\\[\n\\mathcal LJ_{h,q,\\delta}(F_t)\\leq0.\n\\]\nThe trace identities in Proposition 5 yield (5.9), and its determinant identity gives the second assertion. The dilation argument in the proof of Proposition 2 gives \\(A_q(f)\\geq-d(f)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Proposition 6.1, Uniform Ricci-disc bound", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is \\(C\\geq0\\), depending only on the fixed metric \\(k\\), such that \\( P_{R_k}(f)\\leq C \\) for every finite-area holomorphic disc \\(f:\\mathbb D\\to X\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(X\\) is a compact connected Kähler manifold containing no nonconstant entire curve.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(k\\) is a fixed Kähler metric, \\(R_k=-i\\partial\\bar\\partial\\log\\det k\\), and \\(D\\) is the uniform bound \\(d(f)\\leq D\\) from Lemma 1.1.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(P_{R_k}\\) is the Poisson functional defined in Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(C\\geq0\\), depending only on the fixed metric \\(k\\), such that\n\\[\nP_{R_k}(f)\\leq C\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\nfor every finite-area holomorphic disc \\(f:\\mathbb D\\to X\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(0&lt;\\delta\\leq1\\) and prescribed center \\(x\\), Proposition 1 supplies a maximizer \\(f\\) of\n\\[\nJ_\\delta(f)=d(f)+P_{R_k}(f)-\\delta A_k(f).\n\\]\nUse its boundary-normalized frame \\(B\\) and set\n\\[\nN(z)=B(z)^*k(f(z))B(z),\\qquad\nM=\\sup_X|\\operatorname{tr}_kR_k|.\n\\]\nProposition 6 gives\n\\[\nA_{R_k}(f)\\geq-d(f)\\geq-D\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}N(0)\n\\leq \\delta\\bigl(n-A_{R_k}(f)\\bigr)+M\n\\leq n+D+M.\n\\]\nIts determinant identity and the arithmetic–geometric mean inequality yield\n\\[\nP_{R_k}(f)=\\log\\det N(0)\n\\leq n\\log\\left(\\frac{n+D+M}{n}\\right)=:C_1.\n\\]\nSince \\(A_k(f)\\geq0\\) and \\(d(f)\\leq D\\), the maximal value is at most \\(D+C_1\\), independently of \\(x,\\delta\\).</p><p>For any fixed finite-area disc \\(g\\), perform the maximization at its center:\n\\[\nd(g)+P_{R_k}(g)-\\delta A_k(g)\\leq D+C_1.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\delta\\downarrow0\\) for this fixed disc and use \\(d(g)\\geq0\\). The assertion follows with \\(C=D+C_1\\). No area bound uniform in \\(\\delta\\) is needed for the maximizing discs.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition 6.2, Bounded positive canonical potentials", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a bounded real-valued function \\(u\\) on \\(X\\) such that \\( T=-R_k+i\\partial\\bar\\partial u\\geq0 \\) is a closed positive \\((1,1)\\)-current.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(X\\) is a compact connected Kähler manifold containing no nonconstant entire curve.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(k\\) is a fixed Kähler metric and \\(R_k=-i\\partial\\bar\\partial\\log\\det k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a local holomorphic frame \\(s_i\\) of \\(L=\\det T^{1,0}X\\), put\n\\[\na_i=\\log|s_i|_k^2.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a bounded real-valued function \\(u\\) on \\(X\\) such that\n\\[\nT=-R_k+i\\partial\\bar\\partial u\\geq0\n\\]\nis a closed positive \\((1,1)\\)-current. Each \\(a_i+u\\) is plurisubharmonic and locally bounded. These functions are local weights of a singular Hermitian metric with nonnegative curvature on \\(K_X\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Let\n\\[\nL=\\det T^{1,0}X,\\qquad\n\\pi:Y=L\\setminus0_X\\to X,\n\\]\nand define\n\\[\n\\ell(s)=\\log|s|_k^2.\n\\]\nThis is smooth and finite-valued on connected \\(Y\\). In a local trivialization \\(s=\\lambda s_i(x)\\),\n\\[\n\\ell(s)=\\log|\\lambda|^2+a_i(x),\\qquad\ni\\partial\\bar\\partial a_i=-R_k.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\lambda\\neq0\\), \\(\\log|\\lambda|^2\\) is pluriharmonic, so\n\\[\ni\\partial\\bar\\partial\\ell=-\\pi^*R_k.\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(v=\\mathcal P\\ell\\), using test discs holomorphic past the closed unit disc. If \\(G(0)=s\\) and \\(f=\\pi\\circ G\\), then \\(f\\) has finite area. Green’s formula gives\n\\[\n\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\ell(G(e^{i\\theta}))\\,d\\theta\n=\\ell(s)-P_{R_k}(f).\n\\]\nProposition 7 bounds this below by \\(\\ell(s)-C\\), while the constant disc gives the upper bound \\(\\ell(s)\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\ell-C\\leq v\\leq\\ell.\n\\tag{6.3}\n\\]\nLemma 8.1 applies despite global unboundedness of \\(\\ell\\); (6.3) excludes the identically \\(-\\infty\\) alternative. Thus \\(v\\) is plurisubharmonic.</p><p>For \\(\\lambda\\in\\mathbb C^*\\), fiber multiplication is a biholomorphism of \\(Y\\) and gives a bijection of the corresponding test discs. Since\n\\[\n\\ell(\\lambda s)=\\ell(s)+\\log|\\lambda|^2,\n\\]\nwe have\n\\[\nv(\\lambda s)=v(s)+\\log|\\lambda|^2.\n\\]\nThus \\(v-\\ell=u\\circ\\pi\\) for a function with \\(-C\\leq u\\leq0\\). Pulling back \\(v\\) by \\(s_i\\) proves that \\(a_i+u\\) is plurisubharmonic; it is locally bounded because \\(a_i\\) is smooth and \\(u\\) bounded. The local currents agree and equal\n\\[\n-R_k+i\\partial\\bar\\partial u.\n\\]</p><p>For the line-bundle sign, if \\(s_i=g_{ij}s_j\\), then\n\\[\na_i-a_j=\\log|g_{ij}|^2.\n\\]\nThe dual frames \\(s_i^*\\) of \\(K_X=L^*\\) satisfy \\(s_i^*=g_{ij}^{-1}s_j^*\\). The prescription\n\\[\n|s_i^*|^2=\\exp(-(a_i+u))\n\\]\nis therefore compatible, and its curvature is \\(i\\partial\\bar\\partial(a_i+u)=T\\geq0\\). It represents \\(2\\pi c_1(K_X)\\), not \\(2\\pi c_1(L)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The function \\(\\mathcal Pb\\) is plurisubharmonic or identically \\(-\\infty\\).", "statement": "<p>(Established Poletsky–Rosay theorem used in Section 6.1; cited as [26, 27, 11].)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(Z\\) is a connected complex manifold.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(b:Z\\to\\mathbb R\\cup\\{-\\infty\\}\\) is upper semicontinuous.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\n\\mathcal Pb(y)=\n\\inf_{G(0)=y}\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2\\pi}b(G(e^{i\\theta}))\\,d\\theta,\n\\]\nwhere \\(G:\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\to Z\\) is continuous and holomorphic on \\(\\mathbb D\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The function \\(\\mathcal Pb\\) is plurisubharmonic or identically \\(-\\infty\\). No compactness of \\(Z\\) or lower bound on \\(b\\) is required.</p><p>If \\(b\\) is continuous and real-valued, the same envelope results when test maps are required to be holomorphic on neighborhoods of \\(\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The plurisubharmonicity assertion is the manifold case of [11, Theorem 1.1], following the disc-envelope results of Poletsky and Rosay [26, 27]. For continuous real-valued \\(b\\), replace a test map \\(G\\) by \\(z\\mapsto G(rz)\\) and let \\(r\\uparrow1\\), proving equality with the envelope using maps holomorphic past the closed disc.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Proposition 6.4, Nefness of the canonical class", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), there is a smooth real-valued function \\(u_\\varepsilon\\) on \\(X\\) such that \\( -R_k+i\\partial\\bar\\partial u_\\varepsilon\\geq-\\varepsilon k. \\) Consequently \\(2\\pi c_1(K_X)\\) is nef.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(X\\) is a compact connected Kähler manifold containing no nonconstant entire curve.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(k\\) is a fixed Kähler metric and \\(R_k=-i\\partial\\bar\\partial\\log\\det k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A holomorphic line bundle is nef if, for every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), it has a smooth Hermitian metric whose real curvature is at least \\(-\\varepsilon k\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), there is a smooth real-valued function \\(u_\\varepsilon\\) on \\(X\\) such that\n\\[\n-R_k+i\\partial\\bar\\partial u_\\varepsilon\\geq-\\varepsilon k.\n\\]\nConsequently \\(2\\pi c_1(K_X)\\) is nef.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 8 supplies a bounded \\(u\\) with\n\\[\n-R_k+i\\partial\\bar\\partial u\\geq0.\n\\]\nApply Lemma 9.2 with \\(M=X\\) and \\(\\alpha=-R_k\\). The resulting smooth functions satisfy the asserted bound. In the dual canonical frames \\(s_i^*\\), the squared norms\n\\[\n\\exp(-(a_i+u_\\varepsilon))\n\\]\ndefine smooth Hermitian metrics with curvature\n\\[\n-R_k+i\\partial\\bar\\partial u_\\varepsilon\\geq-\\varepsilon k.\n\\]\nThus the canonical class is nef.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Lemma A.1, Fixed-factor changes of radius", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(A>1\\) and \\(Ar<R\\), \\( 0\\leq S_x(Ar)-S_x(r) \\leq \\frac{(M-m)\\log A}{\\log(R/r)}, \\qquad x\\in E. \\) For every sufficiently small fixed \\(r\\), the function \\(x\\mapsto S_x(r)\\) is plurisubharmonic on its open …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(\\psi\\) is plurisubharmonic on a domain in \\(\\mathbb C^n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Every closed ball \\(\\overline B(x,R)\\), with \\(x\\) in a fixed set \\(E\\), lies in the domain.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(m\\leq\\psi\\leq M\\) on their union.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nS_x(r)=\\sup_{|z-x|\\leq r}\\psi(z),\\qquad 0&lt;r\\leq R.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(A&gt;1\\) and \\(Ar&lt;R\\),\n\\[\n0\\leq S_x(Ar)-S_x(r)\n\\leq \\frac{(M-m)\\log A}{\\log(R/r)},\n\\qquad x\\in E.\n\\tag{A.1}\n\\]\nFor every sufficiently small fixed \\(r\\), the function \\(x\\mapsto S_x(r)\\) is plurisubharmonic on its open domain of definition.</p>", "proof": "<p>The function \\(t\\mapsto S_x(e^t)\\) is nondecreasing and convex. For \\(0&lt;r_1&lt;r_2\\leq R\\), restrict \\(\\psi\\) to each complex line through \\(x\\) and compare it on\n\\[\nr_1&lt;|z-x|&lt;r_2\n\\]\nwith the harmonic function affine in \\(\\log|z-x|\\) taking the constant values \\(S_x(r_1)\\) and \\(S_x(r_2)\\) on the boundary circles. The subharmonic maximum principle gives the bound on each annulus; on the inner ball the bound follows from monotonicity of the interpolation. Taking the supremum over an intermediate ball proves convexity.</p><p>Apply convexity at \\(\\log r,\\log r+\\log A,\\log R\\):\n\\[\nS_x(Ar)-S_x(r)\n\\leq \\frac{\\log A}{\\log(R/r)}\n\\bigl(S_x(R)-S_x(r)\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe bounds on \\(\\psi\\) prove (A.1).</p><p>For fixed \\(r\\), the supremum over the closed translation ball is upper semicontinuous: for convergent centers, choose maximizing translation vectors and pass to a convergent subsequence in \\(\\overline B(0,r)\\); upper semicontinuity of \\(\\psi\\) gives the upper bound. It is also a locally bounded supremum of the plurisubharmonic translations \\(x\\mapsto\\psi(x+\\zeta)\\), \\(|\\zeta|\\leq r\\). Its upper semicontinuity therefore makes it plurisubharmonic.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.3, Smoothing a bounded positive potential; proved in Appendix A", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), there is a smooth real-valued function \\(u_\\varepsilon\\) on \\(M\\) such that \\( \\alpha+i\\partial\\bar\\partial u_\\varepsilon\\geq-\\varepsilon k. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Relative to Proposition 9, replace \\(X\\) by an arbitrary compact Kähler manifold \\((M,k)\\); hyperbolicity is not assumed.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\alpha\\) is a smooth closed real \\((1,1)\\)-form on \\(M\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(u\\) is a bounded real-valued function such that\n\\[\n\\alpha+i\\partial\\bar\\partial u\\geq0\n\\]\nin the sense of currents.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), there is a smooth real-valued function \\(u_\\varepsilon\\) on \\(M\\) such that\n\\[\n\\alpha+i\\partial\\bar\\partial u_\\varepsilon\\geq-\\varepsilon k.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose finitely many coordinate patches \\(U_i\\), smooth real functions \\(a_i\\) with\n\\[\n\\alpha=i\\partial\\bar\\partial a_i\n\\]\non neighborhoods of \\(\\overline U_i\\), and open sets\n\\[\nV_i\\Subset W_i\\Subset U_i\n\\]\nsuch that the \\(V_i\\) cover \\(M\\). Such local potentials exist because \\(\\alpha\\) is smooth, closed, and of type \\((1,1)\\); their differences are pluriharmonic.</p><p>Positivity means that \\(a_i+u\\) has a unique plurisubharmonic representative \\(\\psi_i\\). Its almost-everywhere bounds follow from boundedness of \\(u\\) and smoothness of \\(a_i\\); the submean inequality and upper semicontinuity extend them to every point. On overlaps,\n\\[\n\\psi_i-\\psi_j=a_i-a_j\n\\]\npointwise, because the relevant plurisubharmonic functions agree almost everywhere. Replacing \\(u\\) on a null set gives \\(\\psi_i=a_i+u\\) everywhere. The \\(\\psi_i\\) are uniformly bounded above and below over this finite collection.</p><p>In \\(U_i\\)-coordinates, put\n\\[\nS_{i,b}(x)=\\sup_{|y-x|\\leq b}\\psi_i(y).\n\\]\nFix a smooth nonnegative kernel of integral one supported in the unit ball, and let \\(\\rho_{b/4}\\) be its rescaling to radius \\(b/4\\). For small \\(b&gt;0\\), define near \\(\\overline W_i\\)\n\\[\n\\varphi_{i,b}=S_{i,b}*\\rho_{b/4}.\n\\]\nLemma 9.1 and convolution show that \\(\\varphi_{i,b}\\) is smooth and plurisubharmonic. If \\(|y-x|\\leq b/4\\), then\n\\[\nB(x,b/2)\\subset B(y,b)\\subset B(x,2b).\n\\]\nTaking suprema and averaging gives\n\\[\nS_{i,b/2}(x)\\leq\\varphi_{i,b}(x)\\leq S_{i,2b}(x).\n\\tag{A.2}\n\\]</p><p>The required overlap comparison is\n\\[\n\\sup_{W_i\\cap W_j}\n|(\\varphi_{i,b}-a_i)-(\\varphi_{j,b}-a_j)|\n\\longrightarrow0\n\\quad\\text{as }b\\downarrow0.\n\\tag{A.3}\n\\]\nCoordinate changes and their inverses have bounded derivatives near the compact sets \\(\\overline W_i\\cap\\overline W_j\\). Thus, for fixed \\(c,C&gt;0\\), an \\(i\\)-coordinate radius-\\(b\\) ball contains the \\(j\\)-coordinate radius-\\(cb\\) ball and lies in the \\(j\\)-coordinate radius-\\(Cb\\) ball. For sufficiently small \\(b\\), all these balls lie in \\(U_i\\cap U_j\\).</p><p>The identity \\(\\psi_i-\\psi_j=a_i-a_j\\), and the uniform \\(O(b)\\) variation of this smooth difference on the balls, give\n\\[\nS_{j,c_1b}(x)-a_j(x)-C_2b\n\\leq\\varphi_{i,b}(x)-a_i(x)\n\\leq S_{j,c_2b}(x)-a_j(x)+C_2b\n\\]\nfor fixed positive constants. The analogous bounds for \\(\\varphi_{j,b}-a_j\\) use radii \\(b/2\\) and \\(2b\\). Lemma 9.1 compares the four suprema with error \\(O(1/|\\log b|)\\). This proves (A.3), with error \\(O(1/|\\log b|)+O(b)\\), uniformly over the finitely many pairs.</p><p>Choose smooth functions \\(\\chi_i\\) near \\(\\overline W_i\\) with\n\\[\n-3\\leq\\chi_i\\leq0,\\qquad\n\\chi_i=0\\text{ on }V_i,\\qquad\n\\chi_i=-3\\text{ near }\\partial W_i.\n\\]\nFor a fixed \\(C_0\\geq0\\),\n\\[\ni\\partial\\bar\\partial\\chi_i\\geq-C_0k.\n\\]\nGiven \\(\\sigma&gt;0\\), choose \\(b\\) so that every overlap discrepancy in (A.3) is less than \\(\\sigma\\), and put\n\\[\nf_i=\\varphi_{i,b}-a_i+\\sigma\\chi_i\n\\quad\\text{on }W_i.\n\\]</p><p>For a compatible regularized maximum, fix a smooth even probability density \\(\\eta_\\sigma\\) supported in \\((-\\sigma/4,\\sigma/4)\\). For each nonempty finite index set \\(I\\), define\n\\[\n\\mathcal M_I((t_i)_{i\\in I})\n=\n\\int_{\\mathbb R^I}\n\\max_{i\\in I}(t_i+s_i)\n\\prod_{i\\in I}\\eta_\\sigma(s_i)\\,ds_i.\n\\]\nThis is smooth, convex, nondecreasing in each argument, and satisfies\n\\[\n\\mathcal M_I(t+c\\mathbf1)=\\mathcal M_I(t)+c.\n\\tag{A.4}\n\\]\nIf one argument is more than \\(\\sigma/2\\) below the maximum of the others, it never attains the shifted maximum. Removing it leaves exactly \\(\\mathcal M_{I\\setminus\\{i\\}}\\), since its unused density integrates to one.</p><p>For \\(x\\in M\\), let \\(I(x)=\\{i:x\\in W_i\\}\\), and define\n\\[\nu_\\sigma(x)=\\mathcal M_{I(x)}((f_i(x))_{i\\in I(x)}).\n\\]\nThis is globally smooth. Indeed, for \\(x_0\\in\\partial W_i\\), choose \\(j\\) with \\(x_0\\in V_j\\). Near \\(x_0\\), within \\(W_i\\), one has \\(\\chi_i=-3,\\chi_j=0\\), so\n\\[\nf_i&lt;f_j-2\\sigma.\n\\]\nThe \\(i\\)-th branch is inactive under all allowed shifts and is removed by the exact deletion property before crossing \\(\\partial W_i\\). Finiteness removes all patch-boundary indices through a point, leaving a smooth local formula.</p><p>On a fixed coordinate patch with local potential \\(a_j\\), each active branch satisfies\n\\[\na_j+f_i=\\varphi_{i,b}+(a_j-a_i)+\\sigma\\chi_i.\n\\]\nBecause \\(a_j-a_i\\) is pluriharmonic,\n\\[\ni\\partial\\bar\\partial(a_j+f_i)\\geq-C_0\\sigma k.\n\\tag{A.5}\n\\]\nBy translation invariance (A.4), \\(a_j+u_\\sigma\\) is the regularized maximum of these branches. The first derivatives of \\(\\mathcal M_I\\) are nonnegative and sum to one; its Hessian is positive semidefinite. The complex Hessian chain rule therefore gives\n\\[\n\\alpha+i\\partial\\bar\\partial u_\\sigma\n=i\\partial\\bar\\partial(a_j+u_\\sigma)\n\\geq-C_0\\sigma k.\n\\]\nChoose \\(C_0\\sigma\\leq\\varepsilon\\) and rename the function \\(u_\\varepsilon\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Proposition 7.1, Uniform lower bound for the volume form", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every smooth Kähler form \\(h\\) satisfying \\(R_h\\geq-h\\) obeys \\( h^n\\geq c_{n,D}k^n,\\qquad c_{n,D}=e^{-D}\\left(\\frac{n}{2n+D}\\right)^n>0. \\) The constant is independent of \\(h\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(X\\) is a compact connected Kähler manifold of dimension \\(n\\geq1\\), containing no nonconstant entire curve.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(k\\) is a fixed Kähler form.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(D&gt;0\\) satisfies\n\\[\nd(f)=k(f'(0),f'(0))\\leq D\n\\]\nfor every holomorphic disc in \\(X\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a smooth Kähler form \\(h\\), set \\(R_h=-i\\partial\\bar\\partial\\log\\det h\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Volume forms \\(h^n,k^n\\) are used without a factor \\(n!\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every smooth Kähler form \\(h\\) satisfying \\(R_h\\geq-h\\) obeys\n\\[\nh^n\\geq c_{n,D}k^n,\\qquad\nc_{n,D}=e^{-D}\\left(\\frac{n}{2n+D}\\right)^n&gt;0.\n\\tag{7.1}\n\\]\nThe constant is independent of \\(h\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix \\(x\\in X\\). Apply Proposition 1 with \\(q=-h,\\delta=1\\), obtaining a maximizer \\(f\\) of\n\\[\nJ(f)=d(f)-P_h(f)-A_h(f).\n\\]\nThe constant disc has value zero, so\n\\[\nP_h(f)+A_h(f)\\leq d(f)\\leq D.\n\\tag{7.2}\n\\]\nBoth left-hand terms are nonnegative. Since \\(R_h\\geq-h\\),\n\\[\nA_{R_h}(f)\\geq-A_h(f)\\geq-D,\\qquad\nP_{R_h}(f)\\geq-P_h(f)\\geq-D.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(B\\) be the boundary-\\(h\\)-unitary frame. Proposition 6, with\n\\(\\operatorname{tr}_h(-h)=-n\\), gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(B(0)^*k(x)B(0))\n\\leq2n-A_{R_h}(f)\\leq2n+D.\n\\]\nIts determinant identity gives\n\\[\n\\det(B(0)^*h(x)B(0))\n=\\exp(P_{R_h}(f))\\geq e^{-D}.\n\\]\nThe arithmetic–geometric mean inequality yields\n\\[\n\\det(B(0)^*k(x)B(0))\n\\leq\\left(\\frac{2n+D}{n}\\right)^n.\n\\]\nTaking the quotient cancels \\(|\\det B(0)|^2\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{h^n}{k^n}(x)\n=\n\\frac{\\det(B(0)^*h(x)B(0))}\n{\\det(B(0)^*k(x)B(0))}\n\\geq\ne^{-D}\\left(\\frac n{2n+D}\\right)^n.\n\\]\nSince \\(x\\) was arbitrary, this proves the assertion.</p><p>The disc, its boundary regularity, and the size of its variation may depend on \\(h,x\\). The identities are applied separately for each choice; none of these auxiliary bounds enters \\(c_{n,D}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Proposition 8.2, Positive top self-intersection", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( \\int_Xc_1(K_X)^n>0. \\) More precisely, \\( (2\\pi)^n\\int_Xc_1(K_X)^n =\\int_X\\alpha^n \\geq c_{n,D}\\int_Xk^n>0. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(X\\) is a compact connected Kähler manifold of dimension \\(n\\geq1\\) containing no nonconstant entire curve.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(k\\) is fixed, and \\(R_k=-i\\partial\\bar\\partial\\log\\det k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\n\\alpha=[-R_k]=2\\pi c_1(K_X).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(c_{n,D}\\) be the constant in Proposition 10.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\int_Xc_1(K_X)^n&gt;0.\n\\]\nMore precisely,\n\\[\n(2\\pi)^n\\int_Xc_1(K_X)^n\n=\\int_X\\alpha^n\n\\geq c_{n,D}\\int_Xk^n&gt;0.\n\\tag{8.3}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>By Proposition 9, for each \\(t&gt;0\\) there is a smooth real \\(a_t\\) with\n\\[\n-R_k+i\\partial\\bar\\partial a_t\\geq-\\frac t2k.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\gamma_t=-R_k+tk+i\\partial\\bar\\partial a_t\n\\geq\\frac t2k&gt;0\n\\]\nis Kähler. Apply Lemma 11.1 with \\(\\gamma_t\\) and\n\\[\n\\Omega_t=e^{a_t}k^n.\n\\]\nThe resulting Kähler form\n\\[\nh_t=\\gamma_t+i\\partial\\bar\\partial w_t\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\nh_t^n=e^{a_t+w_t}k^n.\n\\tag{8.1}\n\\]\nTaking \\(-i\\partial\\bar\\partial\\) of the logarithm of the volume ratio gives\n\\[\nR_{h_t}\n=R_k-i\\partial\\bar\\partial(a_t+w_t)\n=-h_t+tk\\geq-h_t.\n\\tag{8.2}\n\\]\nProposition 10 gives, uniformly for \\(t&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\int_Xh_t^n\\geq c_{n,D}\\int_Xk^n.\n\\]\nSince \\([h_t]=\\alpha+t[k]\\), closedness and Stokes’ theorem give\n\\[\n\\int_Xh_t^n=\\int_X(\\alpha+t[k])^n.\n\\]\nThe right-hand side is a polynomial in \\(t\\). Passing to its value at zero proves (8.3). This uses only cohomology; no convergence of \\(h_t\\), or uniform bound on its potentials, is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "Theorem 8.1, Negative-sign complex Monge–Ampère theorem; an established theorem", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a smooth real function \\(w\\) such that \\( \\gamma+i\\partial\\bar\\partial w>0,\\qquad (\\gamma+i\\partial\\bar\\partial w)^n=e^w\\Omega. \\) No normalization of \\(\\Omega\\) is prescribed; the additive constant of \\(w\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(M\\) is a compact Kähler manifold of dimension \\(n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\gamma\\) is a Kähler form on \\(M\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\Omega\\) is a smooth positive volume form.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a smooth real function \\(w\\) such that\n\\[\n\\gamma+i\\partial\\bar\\partial w&gt;0,\\qquad\n(\\gamma+i\\partial\\bar\\partial w)^n=e^w\\Omega.\n\\]\nNo normalization of \\(\\Omega\\) is prescribed; the additive constant of \\(w\\) enters the right-hand side.</p>", "proof": "<p>This is the negative-sign Aubin–Yau existence theorem [1, 33]. The statement for arbitrary smooth positive volume form is also recorded in [2, §1, Equation (1.1)]. It does not require \\(\\gamma\\) to represent the canonical class.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Lemma 9.1 and Theorem 9.2, Passage from nef positive intersection to bigness and projectivity", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The line bundle \\(L\\) is big, \\(X\\) is Moishezon, and \\(X\\) is projective.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Relative to the hyperbolic setting, let \\((X,k)\\) be any compact connected Kähler manifold of dimension \\(n\\geq1\\); hyperbolicity is not assumed.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(L\\) is a nef holomorphic line bundle on \\(X\\), and\n\\[\n\\int_Xc_1(L)^n&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">A line bundle is big if its Kodaira dimension is \\(n\\), equivalently if its section spaces have growth of order \\(m^n\\) along sufficiently divisible powers.</p><p class=\"li\">A compact complex manifold is Moishezon if its meromorphic function field has transcendence degree \\(n\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The line bundle \\(L\\) is big, \\(X\\) is Moishezon, and \\(X\\) is projective.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 12.1 makes \\(L\\) big and \\(X\\) Moishezon. The assumed Kähler metric then permits application of Lemma 12.2, proving projectivity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "Lemma 9.1, Nefness and positive intersection imply bigness", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The line bundle \\(L\\) is big and \\(X\\) is Moishezon.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(L\\) is a nef holomorphic line bundle on a compact connected Kähler manifold \\((X,k)\\) of dimension \\(n\\geq1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\int_Xc_1(L)^n&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For the real curvature \\(\\Theta\\) of a smooth Hermitian metric on \\(L\\), \\(X(\\Theta,\\leq1)\\) is the locus where \\(\\Theta\\) is nondegenerate and has at most one negative eigenvalue.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The line bundle \\(L\\) is big and \\(X\\) is Moishezon.</p>", "proof": "<p>Demailly’s strong holomorphic Morse inequalities [7, Theorem 0.1 and Equation (0.7)] give the criterion\n\\[\n\\int_{X(\\Theta,\\leq1)}\\Theta^n&gt;0\n\\tag{9.1}\n\\]\nfor bigness and the Moishezon property [7, Theorem 0.8(a)].</p><p>Put\n\\[\n\\beta=2\\pi c_1(L),\\qquad V=\\int_X\\beta^n&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor \\(0&lt;\\varepsilon\\leq1\\), choose a smooth metric with real curvature\n\\(\\Theta_\\varepsilon\\geq-\\varepsilon k\\). Then\n\\[\nP_\\varepsilon=\\Theta_\\varepsilon+2\\varepsilon k\n\\]\nis positive definite.</p><p>At a point where \\(\\Theta_\\varepsilon\\) has a negative eigenvalue, let\n\\(\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_n\\) be its eigenvalues relative to \\(k\\), and choose \\(j\\) with \\(\\lambda_j&lt;0\\). Then\n\\[\n|\\lambda_j|\\leq\\varepsilon,\\qquad\n|\\lambda_i|\\leq\\lambda_i+2\\varepsilon\\quad(i\\neq j).\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n\\left|\\prod_{i=1}^n\\lambda_i\\right|\n\\leq\\varepsilon\\prod_{i\\neq j}(\\lambda_i+2\\varepsilon)\n\\leq\n\\varepsilon\\sum_{j=1}^n\\prod_{i\\neq j}(\\lambda_i+2\\varepsilon).\n\\]\nAs volume forms,\n\\[\n|\\Theta_\\varepsilon^n|\n\\leq n\\varepsilon P_\\varepsilon^{n-1}\\wedge k\n\\tag{9.2}\n\\]\non the entire negative-eigenvalue locus. Its right-hand side has integral\n\\[\nn\\varepsilon\\int_X\n(\\beta+2\\varepsilon[k])^{n-1}\\wedge[k]\n=O(\\varepsilon).\n\\]\nThe constant is independent of the chosen metric, since the cohomological integral is a fixed polynomial bounded for \\(0\\leq\\varepsilon\\leq1\\).</p><p>The degenerate locus contributes zero to \\(\\Theta_\\varepsilon^n\\). Removing all loci of index at least two changes its total integral by at most \\(O(\\varepsilon)\\). Since\n\\(\\int_X\\Theta_\\varepsilon^n=V\\),\n\\[\n\\int_{X(\\Theta_\\varepsilon,\\leq1)}\n\\Theta_\\varepsilon^n\n\\geq V-O(\\varepsilon)&gt;0\n\\]\nfor a sufficiently small fixed \\(\\varepsilon\\). Criterion (9.1) proves the assertion. In dimension one the index-at-least-two locus is empty, and the argument applies with \\(P_\\varepsilon^0=1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.2", "orig": "Theorem 9.2, Moishezon’s projectivity theorem; an established theorem", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The manifold \\(X\\) is projective.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(X\\) is a compact Moishezon manifold admitting a Kähler metric.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The manifold \\(X\\) is projective.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Moishezon’s projectivity theorem. The paper cites the stronger form in [22, Theorem 6 of the author’s preprint], permitting Moishezon spaces with \\(1\\)-rational singularities. A smooth manifold satisfies that hypothesis.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Proposition 9.3, Bigness in the absence of rational curves", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The canonical bundle \\(K_X\\) is ample.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(X\\) is a smooth projective variety over \\(\\mathbb C\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(K_X\\) is big.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(X\\) contains no rational curves.</p><p class=\"li\">Relative to the earlier analytic propositions, projectivity is now assumed; no background Kähler metric or hyperbolicity assumption is needed beyond the stated absence of rational curves.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The canonical bundle \\(K_X\\) is ample.</p>", "proof": "<p>Kodaira’s lemma gives an integer \\(m&gt;0\\), an ample Cartier divisor \\(H\\), and an effective divisor \\(E\\) such that\n\\[\nmK_X\\sim H+E.\n\\tag{9.3}\n\\]\nFor this form of the lemma, choose a smooth very ample hyperplane section \\(H\\). Bigness gives\n\\[\nh^0(X,mK_X)\\geq cm^n\n\\]\nalong sufficiently divisible powers, whereas the weak holomorphic Morse inequality gives\n\\[\nh^0(H,mK_X|_H)=O(m^{n-1})\n\\]\n[7, Theorem 0.1(a)]. The restriction sequence gives a nonzero section of \\(mK_X-H\\) for some \\(m\\), producing \\(E\\). For \\(n=1\\), \\(H\\) is a finite set and the same estimate is \\(O(1)\\).</p><p>Choose a sufficiently small positive rational \\(\\eta\\) so that \\((X,\\eta E)\\) is Kawamata log terminal. This is possible even if \\(E\\) is singular or nonreduced. Choose a log resolution\n\\[\n\\pi:Y\\to X\n\\]\nof \\((X,E)\\), whose existence is cited from [20, Theorem 35], and write\n\\[\nK_Y-\\pi^*K_X=\\sum_i a_iF_i,\\qquad\n\\pi^*E=\\widetilde E+\\sum_i b_iF_i,\n\\]\nwhere the \\(F_i\\) are exceptional prime divisors and \\(\\widetilde E\\) is the strict transform with multiplicities. By the discrepancy criterion [13, §4.4], exceptional discrepancies for \\((X,\\eta E)\\) are \\(a_i-\\eta b_i\\). Here \\(a_i\\geq0\\) because \\(X\\) is smooth, and \\(b_i\\geq0\\) because \\(E\\) is effective. There are finitely many coefficients. Small enough \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) makes every discrepancy greater than \\(-1\\) and every strict-transform boundary coefficient less than one.</p><p>The log cone theorem for a projective Kawamata log terminal pair with effective rational boundary produces a rational curve of negative \\((K_X+\\Delta)\\)-degree whenever \\(K_X+\\Delta\\) is not nef; this is cited from [13, Theorem 1.1, especially part (5)]. The non-log-canonical locus is empty here, and there is no dimension restriction. Since \\(X\\) has no rational curves,\n\\[\nN=K_X+\\eta E\n\\]\nis nef.</p><p>Combining this with (9.3),\n\\[\n(1+\\eta m)K_X\\sim_{\\mathbb Q}N+\\eta H.\n\\]\nA nef rational divisor plus an ample rational divisor is ample by Kleiman’s criterion [13, Definition 4.9 and Theorem 4.10]: it is positive on every nonzero element of the closed cone of curves. Thus \\((1+\\eta m)K_X\\) is ample. Clearing the positive rational coefficient makes a positive integral multiple of \\(K_X\\) ample, and therefore \\(K_X\\) itself is ample.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 2, "cite"], [3, 5, "cite"], [3, 7, "cite"], [3, 10, "cite"], [3, 13, "cite"], [3, 14, "cite"], [3, 20, "cite"], [4, 3, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [4, 14, "cite"], [5, 7, "cite"], [5, 13, "cite"], [6, 5, "cite"], [7, 9, "cite"], [7, 10, "cite"], [7, 13, "cite"], [8, 7, "cite"], [9, 10, "cite"], [9, 13, "cite"], [10, 13, "cite"], [11, 10, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [12, 10, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [13, 20, "cite"], [14, 15, "cite"], [15, 17, "cite"], [16, 15, "cite"], [17, 0, "cite"], [17, 21, "cite"], [18, 19, "cite"], [19, 17, "cite"], [20, 21, "cite"], [21, 0, "cite"], [22, 21, "cite"], [23, 0, "cite"], [24, 23, "cite"], [25, 23, "cite"], [26, 0, "cite"]]});
